  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how fungi occupy and connect different habitats around plants, especially at the soil and water interfaces. The top scene shows a terrestrial-to-aquatic landscape with a tree, mushrooms releasing aerial spores, shoreline plants, and nearby water, suggesting that fungi disperse widely and link aboveground and belowground environments.\n\nThe lower left inset focuses on the mycorrhizosphere around roots. It highlights fungal associations such as endomycorrhiza and ectomycorrhiza, along with hyphal networks extending through soil. These networks appear to move nutrients and carbon, connect roots to endophytes, and alter local conditions, including pH and soil structure. The image emphasizes fungi as active engineers of the root zone rather than passive inhabitants.\n\nThe lower right inset shows growth at environmental interfaces, especially where land meets water. Fungi seem to colonize plant litter and pore spaces, interact with yeasts, and persist in both air-exposed and aquatic settings. Aerial spores and aquatic spores indicate multiple dispersal strategies. The figure also points to decomposition and enzyme release at hyphal tips, suggesting that fungi drive breakdown of organic matter while bridging terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems. Overall, the message is that fungal networks organize nutrient cycling, decomposition, and habitat connectivity across interfaces.",
